  /// expired.
  Future<ReceivedSlate> receiveSlate({required String slateJson});

  /// `summary(refresh: true)` as a stream: a first event at 0 percent, then one with
  /// `summary` set at 100. A failure ends the stream with its [`WalletError`] as the stream's
  /// error, after the first event.
  Stream<RefreshProgress> refresh();

  /// Tells the wallet's tor that the app is back: call it when the app returns to the
  /// foreground and, with `network_changed`, when the device's network changed (Wi-Fi to
  /// mobile data, or back online). Tor leaves its dormant state, and after a network change
  /// it drops every connection, whose routes may be gone, and builds new ones: sends over
  /// Tor in progress then fail, and the onion stays this wallet's and is republished by tor
  /// itself, so `Ready` is not reported again. A bootstrap in progress gets its full time
  /// again. Returns once tor has taken the commands, in at most about 15 seconds.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked` while the wallet is locked and with `TorNotRunning` when no tor
  /// runs: start it with [`EpicWallet::start_tor`]. A tor that does not answer is ended:
  /// the call fails with `TorNotRunning` and the stream of `start_tor` gets `Failed` with
  /// `Stopped` (or `Start` before the bootstrap), then `Stopped`. While tor is still being
  /// started there is nothing to refresh and the call succeeds.
  Future<void> refreshTor({required bool networkChanged});

  /// Releases a finalized or posted send the network never took, once
  /// `TransactionDetails.releasable` says so: the send is cancelled, its inputs are unlocked
  /// and its change is dropped. The node is asked once more first.
  ///
  /// If another node still holds the transaction and mines it later, the inputs are spent by
  /// it after all, and a new send that spends them fails. A refresh within 10080 blocks of
  /// the release then confirms the send, with a transaction event, and restores its change.
  ///
  /// Fails with `NotReleasable`, with `OnChain` or `InPool` when the node's chain or pool
  /// holds the transaction, and with `NodeUnreachable` when the node cannot be asked.
  Future<void> releaseTransaction({required int txId});

  /// Stops accepting invoices from the key of `address`, and returns whether it was
  /// accepted. Invoices it sent that wait already stay. Fails with `InvalidArgument` for an
  /// address that does not parse on this wallet's chain.
  Future<bool> removeInvoiceSender({required String address});

  /// Posts the stored final transaction of `tx_id` to the node again.
  Future<void> repostTransaction({required int txId});

  /// Delivers the slate of send `tx_id` again, to the wallet listening at `url`, which may
  /// be another address or method than the first attempt, then finalizes and posts as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`] does. Use it after a delivery failed. A wallet that answered
  /// the slate before answers it again the same way.
  ///
  /// Fails with `HttpUrlInvalid` or `PlainHttpNotAllowed` for the URL, `InvalidArgument`
  /// when `tx_id` is not a send waiting for its answer, and otherwise as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`].
  Future<HttpResentTx> resendHttp({
    required int txId,
    required String url,
    required bool allowPlainHttp,
  });

  /// Delivers the stored payment `tx_id` of an invoice again, to the sender's allowed
  /// address, after [`EpicWallet::pay_invoice`] failed with `NotDelivered`. Fails with
  /// `InvalidArgument` for a transaction that is not an undelivered payment of an invoice,
  /// and with `TransportFailed` when the relay fails again.
  Future<void> resendInvoicePayment({required int txId});

  /// Delivers the slate of send `tx_id` again, to the wallet at `onion`, which may be
  /// another address or method than the first attempt, then finalizes and posts as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_tor`] does.
  ///
  /// Fails with `OnionAddressInvalid` or `TorNotRunning` before anything is sent,
  /// `InvalidArgument` when `tx_id` is not a send waiting for its answer, and otherwise as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_tor`].
  Future<HttpResentTx> resendTor({required int txId, required String onion});

  /// Moves the invoice address to the next index and returns it. The old address is
  /// abandoned: messages to it are no longer read, so give the new one to every service.
  Future<String> rotateInvoiceAddress();

  /// Scans the chain for this wallet's outputs from `start_height`, or, by default, from
  /// shortly before the last completed scan (the whole chain after a restore), streaming
  /// progress until an event with `finished`, which carries its `result`. A failure
  /// ends the stream with its [`WalletError`] as the stream's error; a cancelled scan ends it
  /// with `Cancelled`.
  ///
  /// With `delete_unconfirmed` the scan also runs the repair of `epic-wallet scan
  /// --delete_unconfirmed` and always starts from the beginning of the chain, so
  /// `start_height` must be `None`. Every output locked by a transaction in progress that is
  /// found unspent on chain is unlocked, and every output not on chain yet is deleted; the
  /// transactions they belong to are cancelled as `cancel_transaction` cancels them. That
  /// includes sends waiting for an answer and payments signed but not yet confirmed: a
  /// payment already on its way may then fail. If one is still mined, its output is not lost,
  /// but it stays hidden until the next scan finds it. The repair covers only the outputs
  /// that were locked or unconfirmed when the scan started; sends and receives made while it
  /// runs are left alone.
  ///
  /// Every page of outputs is stored as it is scanned, so a cancelled or failed scan leaves
  /// the wallet consistent, with what it found so far stored. The next scan with the same
  /// start and the same `delete_unconfirmed` resumes after the last stored page; the repair
  /// of unconfirmed outputs runs only when a scan completes.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for `delete_unconfirmed` with a `start_height`, and with
  /// `WalletBusy` while another scan of this wallet runs.
  Stream<ScanProgress> scan({
    BigInt? startHeight,
    required bool deleteUnconfirmed,
  });

  /// Encrypts `payload` as a `WFEH` version 1 file, the format of the app's `.wfeh` history
  /// backups. Every call draws a fresh salt and nonce, so two files of one payload differ.
  /// Only this wallet, or one restored from the same recovery phrase, opens the file.
  ///
  /// Fails with `Locked`, and with `InvalidArgument` for a payload above 32 MiB.
  Future<Uint8List> sealAppData({required List<int> payload});

  /// Sends to `<address>@<relay>[:port]` over Epicbox. The listener or a drain finalizes and
  /// posts it when the answer arrives. Fails with `InvalidArgument`, storing nothing, for an
  /// address that [`validate_epicbox_address`] refuses, such as a key without `@<relay>`.
  /// `message` and `options` work as in [`EpicWallet::create_send_slate`].
  Future<SentTx> sendEpicbox({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String address,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// [`EpicWallet::create_send_slate`] writing the slate to the file at `path`.
  Future<SentTx> sendFile({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String path,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// Sends `amount` to the wallet listening at `url`, `http(s)://host[:port][/path]`, then
  /// finalizes its answer and posts the transaction. A missing port is 3415 for `http` and
  /// 443 for `https`. `message` is sent as [`EpicWallet::create_send_slate`] sends it.
  ///
  /// Plain `http` to any host but this device needs `allow_plain_http`, set only after the
  /// user agreed that the slate travels unencrypted.
  ///
  /// Fails, storing nothing, with `HttpUrlInvalid` or `PlainHttpNotAllowed` for the URL and
  /// the errors of [`EpicWallet::estimate_fee`]. Fails with `TransportFailed` when the other
  /// wallet could not be reached, refused, or answered with something else than a slate: the
  /// send is then stored as sent with its inputs locked, so deliver it again with
  /// [`EpicWallet::resend_http`] or cancel it with [`EpicWallet::cancel_transaction`]. Fails
  /// with the errors of [`EpicWallet::finalize_and_post`] except `NotPosted` when the answer
  /// could not be finalized; the send then stays sent, and a resend gets the same answer
  /// again.
  Future<HttpSentTx> sendHttp({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String url,
    required bool allowPlainHttp,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// Sends `amount` to the wallet at `onion` over the wallet's tor, then finalizes its
  /// answer and posts the transaction, as [`EpicWallet::send_http`] does. `onion` is read as
  /// [`validate_onion_address`] reads it; the other wallet is always reached at port 80.
  /// Emits `Sending` on the tor stream once the wallet has checked it can pay; an onion that
  /// is not online takes up to about 150 seconds to fail.
  ///
  /// Fails, storing nothing, with `OnionAddressInvalid` for the address, `TorNotRunning`
  /// when tor has not bootstrapped, and the errors of [`EpicWallet::estimate_fee`]. Fails
  /// with `OnionUnreachable` when the other wallet is not online or could not be reached in
  /// time, and with `TransportFailed` when it refused or the exchange broke: the send is then
  /// stored as sent with its inputs locked, so deliver it again with
  /// [`EpicWallet::resend_tor`] or [`EpicWallet::resend_http`], or cancel it. A tor that
  /// stops during the send fails it the same way with `TorNotRunning`. Fails as
  /// [`EpicWallet::send_http`] does when the answer could not be finalized.
  ///
  /// `options` chooses the inputs and change, `Smallest` with one change output when `None`;
  /// change outputs outside 1..=10 fail with `InvalidArgument`.
  Future<HttpSentTx> sendTor({
    required BigInt amount,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
    required String onion,
    String? message,
    SendOptions? options,
  });

  /// Sets the address index of the invoice address, for example to the one the app kept
  /// before the wallet was restored from its phrase, which does not record it. Messages to
  /// the previous invoice address are no longer read. Fails with `InvalidArgument` for 0,
  /// the payments address.
  Future<void> setInvoiceAddressIndex({required int index});

  /// Moves the open wallet to the node and Epicbox relay `config` names, keeping it unlocked.
  /// Calls running when it starts end first, and calls made while it waits or runs start
  /// after it; a running scan is waited for too, so cancel it first to switch at once. A
  /// running listener moves to a new relay once the message in hand is settled and
  /// acknowledged, at most 30 seconds later, and its stream goes on without `Stopped`. A new
  /// relay changes the wallet's Epicbox address to `<key>@<new relay>`; answers sent to the
  /// old address stay on the old relay.
  ///
  /// `config.data_dir` and `config.chain` must be those the wallet was opened with: another
  /// chain is another wallet. Fails with `InvalidArgument`, keeping the servers in use, for
  /// a node URL or relay that is not one, for another directory or chain, and when
  /// `config` turns Epicbox on or off; with `Locked` on a locked wallet.
  Future<void> setServers({required WalletConfig config});

  /// Spends exactly the outputs `commitments` names, by `WalletOutput.commitment`, into one
  /// output of this wallet, as [`EpicWallet::consolidate_outputs`] does, and locks nothing
  /// else. Use it to make a cancelled payment final: once a payment is cancelled its inputs
  /// are spendable here, but the other wallet may still hold a signed copy that spends them,
  /// for example the issuer of an invoice that was paid, until they are spent elsewhere.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` when `commitments` is empty, or names an output that is
  /// unknown, not unspent with `minimum_confirmations`, or named twice; with `NotEnoughFunds`
  /// when the fee takes their whole value; and otherwise as
  /// [`EpicWallet::consolidate_outputs`] does, storing nothing.
  Future<SelfSentTx> spendOutputsToSelf({
    required List<String> commitments,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
  });

  /// Splits the wallet's funds into `outputs` outputs of nearly equal value, from 2 to 10, so
  /// that several sends can follow without waiting for each other's change: a normal network
  /// transaction to the wallet itself, confirmed as any send, never through its Epicbox
  /// address. It spends every output with `minimum_confirmations`, so the whole balance is
  /// locked until it confirms; a wallet with more than 500 spendable outputs spends the 500
  /// smallest and keeps the rest spendable. It shows as two transactions over transport
  /// `self`, a send holding the change outputs and a receive holding one output; the fee is
  /// that of [`EpicWallet::estimate_split`] at the same tip.
  ///
  /// Fails with `InvalidArgument` for a count outside 2..=10, with `NotEnoughFunds` when the
  /// spendable outputs cannot pay the fee and one base unit into each output, and otherwise
  /// as [`EpicWallet::estimate_fee`], storing nothing. Fails with `NotPosted` when it was
  /// stored but the node did not take it: post the send again with
  /// [`EpicWallet::repost_transaction`] or cancel it with
  /// [`EpicWallet::cancel_transaction`].
  Future<SelfSentTx> splitOutputs({
    required int outputs,
    required BigInt minimumConfirmations,
  });

  /// Starts the HTTP listener on `port` and streams what it does until
  /// [`EpicWallet::stop_http_listener`], [`EpicWallet::lock`] or the wallet is dropped. Port
  /// 0 lets the system pick one, which the `Started` event and
  /// [`EpicWallet::http_listener_status`] report; `epic-wallet` listens on 3415.
  ///
  /// The first event is `Started`, `PortInUse` or `BindFailed`; after a start the last one
  /// is `Stopped`. A wallet runs one HTTP listener: a call while one runs or starts gets a
  /// stream that ends at once. On a locked wallet the stream ends with `Locked` as its error
  /// and no event.
  Stream<HttpListenerEvent> startHttpListener({
    required HttpExposure exposure,
    required int port,
  });

  /// Starts the Epicbox listener, unless it runs already, and streams what it does until
  /// [`EpicWallet::stop_listener`]. The last event is `Stopped`; a listener that fails while
  /// running reports an `Error` event first. When the listener cannot start, for example
  /// with `Unsupported` when the configuration names no relay, the stream ends with that
  /// [`WalletError`] as its error and no event. A second call while the listener runs gets
  /// a stream that ends at once.
  Stream<ListenerEvent> startListener();

  /// Starts the wallet's tor from the binary at `tor_binary`, with `lib_dir` on the library
  /// path for the libraries the Tor Expert Bundle ships on Linux, and streams what it does
  /// until [`EpicWallet::stop_tor`], [`EpicWallet::lock`] or the wallet is dropped. Tor keeps
  /// its files in `tor` inside the wallet's data directory.
  ///
  /// The first event is `Starting`, or `Failed` with `Listener` when the wallet's listener
  /// could not start; the last one is always `Stopped`. A wallet runs one tor: a call while one
  /// runs or starts gets a stream that ends at once. On a locked wallet the stream ends with
  /// `Locked` as its error and no event. A call right after a stop or lock waits for the tor
  /// stopped last to be down, as both use the same files; when that takes over 30 seconds,
  /// the stream gets `Failed` with `Start`, then `Stopped`. A tor that an earlier run of the
  /// app left running on the wallet's files, as after the app was killed, is halted first.
  /// The onion is the same on every start. On iOS nothing is started: the stream gets
  /// `Failed` with `Missing`, then `Stopped`.
  Stream<TorEvent> startTor({required String torBinary, String? libDir});

  /// Stops the HTTP listener. Requests in progress finish; its stream ends with `Stopped`
  /// once the port is free.
  void stopHttpListener();

  /// Stops the listener. Its stream ends with `Stopped` shortly after.
  void stopListener();

  /// Stops the wallet's tor, then its listener. The stream of `start_tor` ends with
  /// `Stopped` once both are down. Sends over Tor in progress fail.
  void stopTor();

  /// The default account's balance. With `refresh` it first updates outputs and
  /// transactions from the node. Without, it answers from what the wallet stored at its last
  /// refresh or scan, without the node, also before the first refresh of this handle, with
  /// `validated_with_node` false.
  Future<WalletSummary> summary({required bool refresh});

  /// Expires what invoices left unpaid; run it from time to time, for example after a drain.
  /// Waiting and declined invoices received more than `expiry_secs` ago are deleted, and
  /// invoices this wallet issued over Epicbox that long ago and not paid are cancelled, each
  /// with an `InvoiceExpired` event. Payments of invoices whose time to live passed with the
  /// transaction neither on chain nor in the node's pool are listed in `cancellable`,
  /// unchanged.
  ///
  /// `expiry_secs` defaults to a day and may be at most two days. Fails with
  /// `InvalidArgument` for 0 or more than two days, `NodeUnreachable` when a payment past its
  /// time to live cannot be looked up, and as `cancel_transaction` does.
  Future<InvoiceSweep> sweepInvoices({BigInt? expirySecs});

  TorStatus torStatus();

  /// The transaction `tx_id`, from the store alone. Fails with `InvalidArgument` when the
  /// wallet holds no such transaction.
  Future<TransactionDetails> transaction({required int txId});

  /// Every transaction of the default account, newest first, from the store alone.
  Future<List<TransactionDetails>> transactions();
}

/// A wallet created by [`EpicWallet::create_with_pin`], and its wallet password sealed under the
/// PIN and the PIN key in the app's store. Store `sealed_password`;
/// [`EpicWallet::open_with_pin`] needs it. With the store's contents it allows guessing the
/// PIN offline, so keep it where the store is, not in backups.
class PinWallet {
  final EpicWallet wallet;
  final String sealedPassword;

  const PinWallet({required this.wallet, required this.sealedPassword});

  @override
  int get hashCode => wallet.hashCode ^ sealedPassword.hashCode;

  @override
  bool operator ==(Object other) =>
      identical(this, other) ||
      other is PinWallet &&
          runtimeType == other.runtimeType &&
          wallet == other.wallet &&
          sealedPassword == other.sealedPassword;
}
